Do not match the CPU interrupt controller

IOService::registerService() finishes with startMatching( options ) on the
caller's thread, and registerCPUInterruptController() is reached from inside
a kext's start -- which is itself already inside a match.  Registering the
controller therefore re enters matching on the same thread, and the panel
answers with SM rec c=2, then M<n>, then SM rec again, without settling.

The controller has no personality to match against; it needs to be in a
parent chain for registerService() and to be announced to the platform expert,
nothing more.  Skip matching when it has no provider and carries that name,
and print SM skip cpuic so the line is visible.

---

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -1086,6 +1086,18 @@ IOService::registerService( IOOptionBits options )
 void
 IOService::startMatching( IOOptionBits options )
 {
+	/* nio: registerService() ends in startMatching() (a few lines above
+	 * 1083), so anything that registers a service from inside a kext's
+	 * start re enters matching on the same thread -- the SM rec c=2 lines.
+	 * The CPU interrupt controller has no driver to match against and only
+	 * needs to be linked and announced, so skip matching for it once, and
+	 * say so. */
+	if (getProvider() == NULL && getName()
+	    && !strcmp(getName(), "IOCPUInterruptController")) {
+		printf("\nnio: SM skip cpuic");
+		return;
+	}
+
 	IOService * provider;
 	UInt32      prevBusy = 0;
 	bool        needConfig;
